package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part story: The Clockmaker's Blueprint. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch47 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_clockmakers_blueprint",
            title = "The Clockmaker's Blueprint",
            tagline = "A young maker finds a set of plans that reconnects a town with its public clock.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Silent Face", listOf(
                    "The clock above Marlowe Square had not chimed for eleven years. Its hands still moved, but they stopped at different minutes whenever the weather changed. When Bisi joined the school's design club, she chose the clock as her research project because everyone used it as a meeting point and nobody knew who maintained it.",
                    "The council's public notice said the mechanism was awaiting assessment. Bisi's teacher warned her not to climb the tower or attempt repairs. Instead, she visited the local museum and found a photograph of the clock's opening ceremony. A man beside the clock held a rolled sheet of paper marked with a gear-shaped stamp."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Maker's Mark", listOf(
                    "The museum guide identified the stamp as belonging to Elias Rowe, a clockmaker who had operated a workshop near the old market. His shop had closed long ago, but a local history group held some of his papers. Bisi and her teammate Musa asked the group whether any public plans for the square clock had survived.",
                    "The group found a catalogue entry for a blueprint, but the paper itself was stored in a climate-controlled archive. They arranged a supervised viewing. The plan showed the clock's original mechanism and a small maintenance panel, but the archivist explained that it was a historical document, not an instruction manual for students. Any repair would belong to qualified specialists."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "A Note in the Margin", listOf(
                    "The archivist noticed a handwritten note beside the drawing: 'Listen for the lower wheel before setting the hands.' It appeared to be a reminder for the original technician. Bisi wondered whether the clock's uneven movement came from a worn component, but she wrote that as a question, not a conclusion.",
                    "A museum record listed the clock's installation team and a later inspection by the council. The final inspection had recommended a replacement part, but the order was never completed after the workshop closed. The students asked the council whether the missing report could be found. The heritage officer agreed to search the maintenance archive."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Unsent Order", listOf(
                    "The council archive contained a purchase request for a replacement wheel, dated shortly before Elias Rowe retired. It had been returned because the supplier's specification did not match the clock's original design. A second request was started but never approved. The clock had continued running with its old component until the mechanism became unreliable.",
                    "Bisi and Musa presented the records to the heritage officer. They did not recommend a part or offer to repair anything. Instead, they asked whether the council could commission an inspection using the original blueprint as historical reference. The officer agreed and contacted a specialist who worked on public clocks."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Clockmaker's Family", listOf(
                    "The history group found a public obituary for Elias and contacted his granddaughter, Mara, through an address listed by the family association. Mara had a photograph of her grandfather in the workshop, standing beside a wooden model of the square clock. She also remembered that he had donated a small tool case to the museum.",
                    "Mara visited the archive and confirmed the handwriting on the blueprint. She explained that Elias had been proud of the clock because it was designed to be maintained locally. He had hoped a future apprentice would learn its history. The museum checked its donation records and found the tool case, still sealed and labelled. It would be displayed, not used for repairs."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "A Specialist's Visit", listOf(
                    "The clock specialist inspected the mechanism with the council's maintenance team. The students watched from the public viewing area and listened as the specialist explained that a bearing had worn unevenly. The historical blueprint helped identify the original design, but the specialist used current measurements and safety procedures to decide what was needed.",
                    "The council approved a repair plan and ordered a compatible replacement component. The work would take several weeks because the mechanism needed careful testing. Bisi asked whether the clock could be made to chime again. The specialist said that was possible only after the mechanism passed inspection and the council confirmed the sound level would be suitable for nearby residents."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "A Different Kind of Time", listOf(
                    "While the repair was underway, the design club created a small exhibit about the clock's history. They included the blueprint reproduction with permission, the workshop photograph, and a timeline of the unfinished purchase order. Musa suggested a display showing how public objects depend on records as much as on materials.",
                    "The heritage officer reviewed the exhibit and corrected one date. The club added a note that the clock's silence had not been caused by one person's mistake; several changes in suppliers and council processes had delayed the work. Bisi liked that explanation because it was accurate and fair. The exhibit was placed in the library so residents could learn about the project."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The First Test", listOf(
                    "The specialist completed the replacement and began a supervised test. The hands moved steadily for an entire afternoon, then continued overnight under monitoring. The council received feedback from nearby residents about the proposed chime schedule. Some wanted the old hourly sound restored, while others preferred a quieter signal.",
                    "The council held a short consultation and chose a gentle chime during daytime hours only. The specialist adjusted the mechanism and documented the settings. Bisi's club updated its exhibit with the test results and the consultation outcome. The students had learned that restoring a landmark meant considering the people who lived around it."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "A Square Full of Voices", listOf(
                    "At the reopening, Mara stood with the specialist and the council team. She spoke about her grandfather's workshop and thanked the students for finding the blueprint. The clock chimed softly at noon, and people in the square looked up. A child asked whether the clock would always keep perfect time.",
                    "The specialist explained that public clocks needed routine checks, and the council published a maintenance schedule. Bisi's club added a QR link to the public history page, where residents could report issues through the proper channel. The clock was working, but the important change was that its care was no longer a mystery."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Next Apprentice", listOf(
                    "The museum opened a small annual workshop about local engineering history. Mara donated a copy of the blueprint and the family photograph, while the original remained safely stored. The council invited the clock specialist to speak with students about training routes into conservation engineering. Bisi signed up to help organise the next session.",
                    "Months later, the clock still marked the square's hours, and its maintenance record was available for anyone to read. Bisi understood the note in the margin differently now. Listening before acting meant learning from evidence, experts, and the people affected. The clockmaker's blueprint had not given students permission to repair a tower; it had given a community a reason to care for its shared landmark."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
